ANTISYMMETRIC between branches would still pass the parity
test, because the test is on the sum, not on the stability.

Recorded so that the drift cannot later be presented as either evidence for or
against (B) depending on which way the pair lands.

--- PAIRING RULE, fixed 23:12 before the n = -1 branch reaches any comparison point ---

The n = +1 branch has TWO frames with a valid W (the others were suppressed by the
phase-probe threshold):

    t = 0.50   W = +1.027   (16/16 probes)   helA = +1  <- early transient
    t = 1.00   W = +0.354   (15/16 probes)   helA = -1  <- settled, and the CONTROL
                                                          value matching the original

The n = -1 branch will also have only some frames valid. That makes "which frame to
pair at" a choice, and a choice made after seeing the numbers is not a test. So it is
fixed here:

  1. PAIR AT t = 1.00 IF the n = -1 branch yields a valid W there. That is the
     settled ring, it is the time at which the completed run took its reading, and
     it is where the helA control reproduces. This is the primary and preferred test.

  2. IF t = 1.00 is unavailable on n = -1, pair at t = 0.50, and REPORT IT AS A
     FALLBACK -- with the caveat that helA there is +1 on the n = +1 branch, i.e. the
     early transient, so a t = 0.50 pairing tests the transient ring and not the
     settled one.

  3. NEVER pair readings from DIFFERENT times. W drifts across frames (+1.027 at
     0.50, +0.354 at 1.00 on the same branch), so a cross-time pair sum would mix
     drift into the parity test and could produce either verdict at will.

  4. If NEITHER time is available on n = -1, the run yields no verdict on reading
     (B) and that is the honest outcome -- the instrument would then be too fragile
     at this probe radius, and the fix is an adaptive probe, not a different pairing.

The verdict criterion itself is unchanged: pair sum small against |W| means (B) is
RESCUED and its earlier falsification was the instrument; pair sum large means (B)
fails honestly and NOW earns the ledger row.

--- ADVERSE OBSERVATION, recorded 23:26 BEFORE the n = -1 pair value exists ---

The n = +1 branch has run to T_MAX. Its helA time series is NOT constant:

    t = 0.50   helA +1   16/16 phase points
    t = 0.75   helA -1   10/16
    t = 1.00   helA -1   15/16   <- the control frame, matches the completed run
    t = 1.25   helA +1    1/16
    t = 1.50   helA -1    8/16

THIS WAS INVISIBLE IN THE COMPLETED RUN, which reported helA only at first ring.
It bears on this evening's verdict at 22:08, which rested reading (A)'s survival on
"integer-valued, therefore immune to the 0.0294% drift asymmetry". That argument is
still true as far as it goes -- a common-mode drift cannot move an integer -- but it
is NOT the same as the observable being stable, and the two were treated as one.

WHAT SURVIVES THE OBSERVATION. The frames disagree, but they are not of equal
quality: 1/16 and 8/16 phase points mean the ring was barely resolved, and the m=1
fit that produces helA degrades with the bin count for the same reason W does. Among
WELL-RESOLVED frames only two exist -- t = 0.50 (16/16, helA +1) and t = 1.00 (15/16,
helA -1) -- and t = 0.50 was independently identified as the early transient before
this was noticed. So the settled, well-resolved value is helA = -1 at t = 1.00,
which is what the completed run reported and what the control reproduces.

WHAT DOES NOT SURVIVE. The claim that (A) is robust *because it is an integer* is
too strong on its own. The honest form: (A)'s -1/+1 flip is read at the settled ring
in the branch's best-resolved frame, it reproduces across two independent harnesses,
and it is immune to the drift -- but it is a snapshot of a quantity that this run
shows moving, and a future run should report helA's full series rather than its
first-ring value.

STATED BEFORE THE PAIR IS KNOWN so it cannot be presented as a caveat discovered
only if the answer is unwelcome. If the n = -1 branch gives helA = +1 at t = 1.00,
the flip stands as reported; if it gives -1, reading (A) has no flip at matched time
and this evening's verdict needs revisiting in full.

================================================================================
BOTH BRANCHES COMPLETE — instrument note for whoever runs this next
================================================================================

Phase-probe yield across all ten frames (both branches, t = 0.50 … 1.50):

    n=+1:  16/16   10/16   15/16    1/16    8/16
    n=-1:  16/16    9/16   15/16    5/16   11/16

USABLE FRAMES (>= 12/16): FOUR OF TEN -- 40% yield. And they are exactly t = 0.50
and t = 1.00 on BOTH branches, with nothing usable elsewhere. The fixed probe
radius of 1.5 healing lengths fails on 60% of frames.

WHY THIS MATTERS AND WHY IT DID NOT INVALIDATE THE RESULT. The verdict was taken
at t = 1.00, which is one of the four good frames on both branches, and the choice
of t = 1.00 was FIXED IN WRITING before either branch reached it (the pairing rule
at 23:12) -- on grounds that had nothing to do with probe yield: it is the settled
ring, the completed run's own reading time, and where the helA control reproduces.
That the good frames happen to include it is luck the pre-registration did not rely
on. Had t = 1.00 failed on the n = -1 branch, the rule said NO VERDICT, and that is
what would have been reported.

THE FIX, for the next run: make the probe radius ADAPTIVE rather than fixed --
scale it to the local core separation, or search outward from each core point until
|psi| exceeds the rejection floor, instead of sampling a fixed 1.5 xi and discarding
whatever falls short. The failures cluster where the ring approaches the axis vortex
(probe points land in another core's depleted region and are rejected), which an
outward search handles naturally and a fixed radius cannot.

DO NOT respond to a low-yield frame by lowering the rejection floor. The floor is
what keeps the probe off other cores, and relaxing it reintroduces exactly the
singular-sampling defect this instrument was built to remove.
